{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-62816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A pipe is filling water into a tank. The rate $r(t)$ at which water enters the tank is given by the function \n\n$$\nr(t)=\\frac{15}{1+7{t}^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in liters per minute, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes since the pipe started running. Initially, there were $60$ liters of water in the tank.\n\nWhich integral gives the quantity of water in the tank $t$ minutes after the inlet started running?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-62816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given the **rate of change** of volume $r(t).$ To find the total volume $V(t)$ after $t$ minutes, we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V(t) & =V(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{15}{1+7{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{15}{1+7{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that our final function is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-62816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cba56cf1a7b991293c51a083f6661a42a9b6b9d65f5f11ef2b5e959e70c8501"} {"question" "q-62816" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-62816" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{15}{1+7{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{60}(60+\\frac{15}{1+7{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{60}\\frac{15}{1+7{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{t}(60+\\frac{15}{1+7{x}^{2}})\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{15}{1+7{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-62816" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "60+{\\int }_{0}^{t}\\frac{15}{1+7{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-62817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The rate at which guests check into a particular hotel one evening is given by the function\n\n$$\nr(t)=3{t}^{2}+2,\n$$\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per hour and $t>0$ is the time in hours since $\\text{6}\\,\\text{p.m}.$\n\nGuests are also checking out of the hotel at a steady rate of $1$ guest per hour.\n\nIf there were $200$ guests in the hotel at $6\\,\\text{p.m},$ how many guests will there be in the hotel at $9\\,\\text{p.m?}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-62817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Guests are entering the hotel at a rate of $3{t}^{2}+2$ guests per hour and are leaving at a rate of $1$ guest per hour. Therefore, the **rate of change** in the number of guests (call it $R(t)$) is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(t) & =r(t)-1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+2-1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the **total number of guests** after $t$ hours (call it $T(t)$), we apply the second fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(t) & =T(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{t}R(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo ensure that $T$ is a function of $t$ only, we use a different variable ($x$) for the integration. \n\nTo find out the number of guests after $3$ hours ($9\\,\\text{p.m}$), we substitute $t=3$ into the above, and evaluate.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(3) & =200+{\\int }_{0}^{3}(3{x}^{2}+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =200+{[{x}^{3}+x]|}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =200+[(27+3)-(0)] \\\\  & =200+30 \\\\  & =230\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-62817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cba56cf1a7b991293c51a083f6661a42a9b6b9d65f5f11ef2b5e959e70c8501"} {"question" "q-62817" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-62817" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "215"] ["math" "227"] ["math" "230"] ["math" "233"] ["math" "240"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-62817" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "230"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-62834" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The total number of participants $S(t)$ registered for an online conference after $t$ days is given by the function\n\n$$\nS(t)=40+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, in days, since the conference was announced. \n\nAt what time is the number of participants at a minimum, and how many participants are registered at this time?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-62834" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find when the number participants is minimized, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(40+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(40)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4t-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}S}{\\text{d}t}=0$:\n\n$$\n4x-16=0\\,⟹\\,t=4\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=4.$ To find out how many participants are registered when $t=4,$ we figure out $S(4)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(4) & =40+{\\int }_{0}^{4}(4x-16)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =40+{[2{x}^{2}-16x]|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =40+[(2(4)^{2}-16(4))-(0)] \\\\  & =40-32 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $8<40$, we conclude that the minimum number of participants is $8$, and this occurs after $4$ days." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-62834" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cba56cf1a7b991293c51a083f6661a42a9b6b9d65f5f11ef2b5e959e70c8501"} {"question" "q-62834" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-62834" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The minimum is $32$ participants  after $0$ days"] ["text" "The minimum is $32$ participants  after $4$ days"] ["text" "The minimum is $40$ participants  after $0$ days"] ["text" "The minimum is $40$ participants  after $4$ days"] ["text" "The minimum is $8$ participants  after $4$ days"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-62834" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The minimum is $8$ participants  after $4$ days"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-140822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Scientists are observing a colony of bugs. The population $P(t)$ of the colony is given by the function\n\n$$\nP(t)=27+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-12)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is the number of bugs, and $t>0$ is the time (in days) since the observation started. \n\nAt what time is the population of the colony a minimum, and what is the number of bugs at this time?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-140822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find when the population is a minimum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(27+{\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-12)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(27)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-12)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}(3{x}^{2}-12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0$ for $t>0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}-12 & =0 \\\\ 3({t}^{2}-4) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =4 \\\\ t & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=2.$\nTo find the number of bugs when $t=2,$ we figure out $P(2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2) & =27+{\\int }_{0}^{2}(3{x}^{2}-12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =27+[{x}^{3}-12x]{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =27+[(8-24)-(0)] \\\\  & =27-16 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $11<27$, we conclude that the minimum population is $11$ bugs, and this happens after $2$ days." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-140822" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cba56cf1a7b991293c51a083f6661a42a9b6b9d65f5f11ef2b5e959e70c8501"} {"question" "q-140822" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-140822" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "The minimum is $11$ bugs after $2$ days"] ["text" "The minimum is $15$ bugs after $4$ days"] ["text" "The minimum is $16$ bugs after $2$ days"] ["text" "The minimum is $27$ bugs after $5$ days"] ["text" "The minimum is $8$ bugs after $4$ days"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-140822" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "The minimum is $11$ bugs after $2$ days"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-350001" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The value $P(t)$ of a painting is given by the function\n\n$$\nP(t)=250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is measured in dollars, and $t>0$ is the time (in years) since the painting was created. \n\nAt what time is the value a minimum, and how much does the painting cost at this time, rounded to the nearest dollar?\n\nThe minimum value is\n$$$\n{{field-1}}\nafter\n{{field-2}}\nyears." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-350001" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find when the value is minimized, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0.$ First, we differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250+{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(250)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{0}^{t}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={3}^{t/4}-27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve $\\frac{\\text{d}P}{\\text{d}t}=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{t/4}-27 & =0 \\\\ {3}^{t/4} & ={3}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{t}{4} & =3 \\\\ t & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo there are two candidates for global extrema, $t=0$ and $t=12.$ To find out the value of the painting when $t=12,$ we figure out $P(12):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(12) & =250+{\\int }_{0}^{12}({3}^{x/4}-27)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =250+{[\\frac{4\\cdot {3}^{x/4}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-27x]|}_{0}^{12} \\\\  & =250+[(\\frac{4\\cdot 27}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}-324)-(\\frac{4\\cdot 1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})] \\\\  & =250-324+\\frac{108-4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & =-74+\\frac{104}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\  & ≈21,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nSince $21<250,$ we conclude that the minimum value is $$\\,21$, and this happens after $12$ years." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-350001" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1cba56cf1a7b991293c51a083f6661a42a9b6b9d65f5f11ef2b5e959e70c8501"} {"question" "q-350001" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-350001" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "21"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-350001" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"} {"question" "q-350001" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14071355/state.json" "file_sha256" "15d63dcfaf59049f136afff1e13811e01fe778ddcd666bdb5c5721d38e694a0d"}] "usage" {"q-62817" []} "basis" 2551 "retractions" [[17592186147960 :question/problem "> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nThe rate at which guests check into a particular hotel one evening is given by the function\n\n$r(t) = 3t^{2} + 2$,\n\nwhere $r(t)$ is measured in guests per hour and $t > 0$ is the time in hours since $6p.m$.\n\nGuests are also checking out of the hotel at a steady rate of $1$ guest per hour.\n\nIf there were $200$ guests in the hotel at $6p.m$, how many guests will there be in the hotel at $9p.m$?"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186145772 17592186145773 17592186145774 17592186145775 17592186145776] "immutable_fields" [[17592186145771 :answer-field/correct] [17592186145771 :answer-field/id] [17592186145771 :answer-field/key] [17592186145771 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
